title: A burglar alarm
mode: vhdl
chapter: Combining gates
check: behaves
---- intro
A real problem now. The alarm `y` should sound when:

- the alarm is **armed** and the **door** opens, or
- someone presses the **panic** button, whatever the rest is doing.

Turn the words into one line. Brackets keep "armed and door" together.
---- task
Make `y` 1 when the door opens while armed, or when panic is pressed.
---- start
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity alarm is
  port(
    armed : in  std_logic;
    door  : in  std_logic;
    panic : in  std_logic;
    y     : out std_logic
  );
end alarm;

architecture rtl of alarm is
begin
    -- write it here
end rtl;
---- answer
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity alarm is
  port(
    armed : in  std_logic;
    door  : in  std_logic;
    panic : in  std_logic;
    y     : out std_logic
  );
end alarm;

architecture rtl of alarm is
begin
    y <= (armed and door) or panic;
end rtl;
---- hint1
The words say it: (armed AND door) OR panic.
---- hint2
`y <= (armed and door) or panic;`
---- solution
`y <= (armed and door) or panic;` VHDL will not let you leave the brackets out when `and` and `or` are mixed: it makes you say which comes first.
---- done
Reading the problem out loud and writing down the ANDs and ORs as they come is how most circuits start.
